{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently predicts smooth, mean-reverting paths (e.g., +0.1 to +0.5 EUR/day) while true outcomes exhibit sharp spikes (e.g., +8 to +10 EUR jumps) or deep crashes (e.g., -10 EUR drops). Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges when sentiment history shows mixed or rapidly shifting signals (e.g., alternating 1.00 and 0.00).",
    "Address structural lag in trend reversal: The model fails to anticipate regime shifts, maintaining a flat or slightly upward trajectory even after history shows a clear downward trend (e.g., Nov 2023). When the last 5 days of history show a consistent directional move, force the forecast to align with that momentum rather than reverting to the long-term mean.",
    "Adjust for overshoot after spikes: In cases where the true price spikes (e.g., April 2022), the model's subsequent forecasts remain too low, failing to capture the new higher price floor. If a true outcome exceeds the forecast by >5 EUR, immediately reset the baseline for the next 10 days to the new high rather than the historical average.",
    "Incorporate sentiment magnitude into trend strength: High sentiment scores (1.00) combined with rising prices should trigger an upward acceleration in the forecast, not a flat line. Conversely, low sentiment (0.00) combined with falling prices should trigger a steeper decline. The current model treats sentiment as a binary trigger rather than a magnitude scaler.",
    "Reduce mean reversion bias in the 15-30 day horizon: The model assumes prices will return to ~77-79 EUR regardless of recent extremes. In bull markets (April 2022), extend the forecast horizon to reflect sustained higher levels (85-90 EUR) rather than reverting to the pre-spike mean."
  ]
}
